uages":126},"Latin","la",[124],{"type":128,"bibl":129},"edition",[130],{"author":131,"date":132,"citedRange":133,"ptr":134,"inscriptionDate":132,"year":136},"Gammella","2025","134-145",{"target":135},"https://www.zotero.org/groups/382445/items/TVJFIWIM","",{"type":138,"bibl":139},"discussion",[17],{"editor":141,"principal":144,"contributors":145,"change":149},{"_":142,"ref":143},"Jonathan Prag","#JP",{"_":142,"ref":143},[146],{"_":142,"xml:id":147,"ref":148},"JP","http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3819-8537",{"_":150,"when":151,"who":143},"Jonathan Prag created file from publication","2026-05-04",[153,155],{"atLeast":154,"atMost":154},25,{"atLeast":156,"atMost":156},15,[158],"Gammella, 2025",[160],"TVJFIWIM",[162,163,164,165,25,29,166,167,168,169,167,170,171,125],"isic002133","fragmentary latin inscription",{"_":5},"honorific","agrigentum","agrigento","sicilia","sicily","museo regionale archeologico pietro griffo","latin",[],{"title":174,"body":175,"divs":176,"editions":201,"images":211,"handnote":212},"ISic002133. Fragmentary Latin inscription","\u003Cdiv id=\"editions\">\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Jonathan Prag\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-interpretive\" lang=\"la\">  \u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]M · P̣ẸṬ \u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>  \u003Cbr id=\"al2-interpretive\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan data-text=\"us\">us\u003C/span>  \u003Cspan class=\"tall\" title=\"tall character: f\">f̣\u003C/span>ẹ\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>  \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Jonathan Prag\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-diplomatic\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-diplomatic\">\u003Ca id=\"al1-diplomatic\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]M·...&nbsp;&nbsp; \u003Cbr id=\"al2-diplomatic\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan data-text=\"us\">US\u003C/span>\u003Cspan class=\"tall\" title=\"tall character: f\">.\u003C/span>.&nbsp;&nbsp; \u003C/span>\u003C/div>\u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"facsimile-images\">\u003Ch2>Facsimile Images\u003C/h2>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"apparatus\">\u003Ch4>Apparatus criticus\u003C/h4>\u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan>\u003Cspan>Text after Gammella\u003C/span>\u003C/span>; \u003C/li>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan class=\"1\">1: \u003Cspan>Gammella reads: M(arcus) · Peṭ-; although the M may be considered certain, the following three letters are very closely spaced and it seems impossible to be certain of the reading.\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003Cbr>\u003C/li>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan class=\"2\">2: \u003Cspan>Gammella reads: [--]us fe-\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"translation\">\u003Ch2>English translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">\u003Ca href=\"http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3819-8537\">Jonathan Prag\u003C/a>\u003C/p>  \u003Cp>  \u003C/p>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"commentary\">\u003Ch2>commentary\u003C/h2>  \u003Cp>Gammella interprets as probably a Severan period honorific, given the use of marble and the findspot in the vicinity of the principal public spaces of the city. This seems likely, although such a plaque could also be from a funerary text; but the findspot, albeit seemingly in secondary deposition, encourages the reading as a public text. For the archaeological context, see Rignanese, Giuseppe, and Federico Figura, ‘5. Agrigento. Lo scavo nella terrazza meridionale dell’ekklesiasterion (Saggio EK2)’, ANNALI SCUOLA NORMALE SUPERIORE - CLASSE DI LETTERE E FILOSOFIA, 30 December 2025, pp. 82–104, doi:10.2422/3035-3769.202502_s06. The reading of both lines would seem to be far from certain, with E and F not readily distinguishable, and the lower portions of E, F, T, L,. P and I all sharing considerable similarities in texts of this date. The absence of an interpunct in line 2, while hardly definitive, further complicates the reading with the vacat at the end, and other interpretations are possible (such as f(ilius) f(ecit) or v(otum) s(olvit) fe(liciter)).\u003C/p>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"bibliography\">\u003Ch2>bibliography\u003C/h2>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> Gammella 2025 134-145 \u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"handnote\"> \u003Cp>Relatively tall and narrow letters, with serifs formed by simple extension of strokes, or by thin cross strokes on M and S. Both M and V appear relatively broad.\u003C/p> Lines 1-2  25  Line 1-2  15  \u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"license\"> Licensed under a Creative Commons-Attribution 4.0 licence.  \u003C/div>",[177,180,183,186,189,192,195,198],{"id":178,"html":179},"editions","\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Jonathan Prag\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-interpretive\" lang=\"la\">  \u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]M · P̣ẸṬ \u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>  \u003Cbr id=\"al2-interpretive\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan data-text=\"us\">us\u003C/span>  \u003Cspan class=\"tall\" title=\"tall character: f\">f̣\u003C/span>ẹ\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>  \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Jonathan Prag\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-diplomatic\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-diplomatic\">\u003Ca id=\"al1-diplomatic\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]M·...&nbsp;&nbsp; \u003Cbr id=\"al2-diplomatic\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan data-text=\"us\">US\u003C/span>\u003Cspan class=\"tall\" title=\"tall character: f\">.\u003C/span>.&nbsp;&nbsp; \u003C/span>\u003C/div>\u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>",{"id":181,"html":182},"facsimile-images","\u003Ch2>Facsimile Images\u003C/h2>",{"id":184,"html":185},"apparatus","\u003Ch4>Apparatus criticus\u003C/h4>\u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan>\u003Cspan>Text after Gammella\u003C/span>\u003C/span>; \u003C/li>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan class=\"1\">1: \u003Cspan>Gammella reads: M(arcus) · Peṭ-; although the M may be considered certain, the following three letters are very closely spaced and it seems impossible to be certain of the reading.\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003Cbr>\u003C/li>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan class=\"2\">2: \u003Cspan>Gammella reads: [--]us fe-\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>",{"id":187,"html":188},"translation","\u003Ch2>English translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">\u003Ca href=\"http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3819-8537\">Jonathan Prag\u003C/a>\u003C/p>  \u003Cp>  \u003C/p>",{"id":190,"html":191},"commentary","\u003Ch2>commentary\u003C/h2>  \u003Cp>Gammella interprets as probably a Severan period honorific, given the use of marble and the findspot in the vicinity of the principal public spaces of the city. This seems likely, although such a plaque could also be from a funerary text; but the findspot, albeit seemingly in secondary deposition, encourages the reading as a public text. For the archaeological context, see Rignanese, Giuseppe, and Federico Figura, ‘5. Agrigento. Lo scavo nella terrazza meridionale dell’ekklesiasterion (Saggio EK2)’, ANNALI SCUOLA NORMALE SUPERIORE - CLASSE DI LETTERE E FILOSOFIA, 30 December 2025, pp. 82–104, doi:10.2422/3035-3769.202502_s06. The reading of both lines would seem to be far from certain, with E and F not readily distinguishable, and the lower portions of E, F, T, L,. P and I all sharing considerable similarities in texts of this date. The absence of an interpunct in line 2, while hardly definitive, further complicates the reading with the vacat at the end, and other interpretations are possible (such as f(ilius) f(ecit) or v(otum) s(olvit) fe(liciter)).\u003C/p>  ",{"id":193,"html":194},"bibliography","\u003Ch2>bibliography\u003C/h2>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> Gammella 2025 134-145 \u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  ",{"id":196,"html":197},"handnote"," \u003Cp>Relatively tall and narrow letters, with serifs formed by simple extension of strokes, or by thin cross strokes on M and S. Both M and V appear relatively broad.\u003C/p> Lines 1-2  25  Line 1-2  15  ",{"id":199,"html":200},"license"," Licensed under a Creative Commons-Attribution 4.0 licence.  ",[202,204,207,208],{"html":203},"Text encoding: Jonathan Prag",{"id":205,"lang":125,"html":206},"edition-interpretive","\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]M · P̣ẸṬ \u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>  \u003Cbr id=\"al2-interpretive\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan data-text=\"us\">us\u003C/span>  \u003Cspan class=\"tall\" title=\"tall character: f\">f̣\u003C/span>ẹ\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>  \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>",{"html":203},{"id":209,"lang":125,